course: dictionary
lesson: 7816
title: Money words and slang
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are slang, which means informal words people use in everyday talk, often in one country or region. «It costs five quid» is British and Irish slang for five pounds. You will not find quid in a maths book, but you may hear it in real speech.
explain: The words around a word are its context, and they give you clues. «Ten quid» has a number in front of it and money is being talked about, so quid must mean a unit of money. A «tenner» is £10 and a «fiver» is £5, so these words belong together. Try swapping in pound and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Some phrases use quid in a different way. «Quids in» means you make a profit or are lucky, so you end up with more than you started with. Look for clues such as profit or lucky before you decide what the phrase means.
fact: Quid is informal slang for a pound, the money (£1).
fact: «Ten quid» means £10, and «a tenner» is another way to say £10.
fact: «Quids in» means making a profit or being lucky.
fact: «Not the full quid» is Australian slang meaning not very clever.
fact: Quid also means a lump of chewed tobacco, but this meaning is rare, so you will almost never meet it.
exercise: «It costs five quid to get in.» What does quid mean here? | *A pound in money (£1) | A ticket for the show | A penny, the smallest coin | A piece of chewing tobacco
exercise: Grandma says, «Twenty quid for that?» How much money is that? | *£20 | 20p | £2 | £200
exercise: «We got a free ticket, so we're quids in!» What does quids in mean? | Going shopping for shoes | Counting coins for an hour | *Making a profit or being lucky | Losing all our money
test: «My mate says he will lend me a tenner.» How much money is a tenner? | *£10 | £1 | £5 | £100
test: In Australia, someone who is «not the full quid» is: | a bit of chewing tobacco | very rich | *not very clever | a pound short of a fiver
exam: Ella reads «a fiver and two quid», so how much money is that in total? | £10 | £3 | £52 | *£7
